## **Technical Details**

**Note:** This advisory uses the [MITRE ATT&CK® Matrix for Enterprise](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/matrices/enterprise/) framework, version 20. See[**MITRE ATT&CK Tactics and Techniques**](https://www.cisa.gov/cybersecurity-advisories/all.xml#MITRE) section of this advisory for a table of the threat actors’ activity mapped to MITRE ATT&CK tactics and techniques.

### **Initial Access**

The FBI, USSS, and cybersecurity researchers observed SafePay threat actors obtaining initial access [[TA0001](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/tactics/TA0001/ "TA0001")] to organizations through various means, including credential exposure, implementing brute force password attacks [[T1110](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1110/ "T1110")], and exploiting virtual private network (VPN) access. SafePay threat actors use the IP addresses of the VPN provider “Mullvad” to attain initial access—specifically, SafePay threat actors use phished account profiles to access victim networks to exploit the following known Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs) for edge device products:

*   [CVE-2020-3259](https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2020-3259) [[Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)-200: Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)]
*   [CVE-2023-20269](https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2023-20269) [[CWE-288: Authentication Bypass Using an Alternate Path or Channel](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/288.html)]

Additionally, the authoring agencies observed SafePay threat actors exploiting the following vulnerabilities across internet-exposed systems [[T1190](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1190/)] for initial access through automated scripts:

*   [CVE-2023-27532](https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2023-27532 "CVE-2023-27532") [[CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html "CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function")]
*   [CVE-2024-40711](https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2024-40711 "CVE-2024-40711") [[CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502 "CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data")]
*   [CVE-2024-40766](https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2024-40766 "CVE-2024-40766") [[CWE-284: Improper Access Control](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/284.html)]

### **Execution, Persistence, Stealth, and Defense Impairment**

After SafePay threat actors gain access to a victim network, they establish persistence by gaining administrative privileges on existing accounts [[T1098](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1098/)], or on newly created accounts [[T1136](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1136/)], using built-in administrative tools consistent with [living off the land (LOTL)](https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/identifying-and-mitigating-living-land-techniques) techniques. Once SafePay threat actors establish a foothold in a victim’s network, they use LOTL binaries (LOLBins) [[T1218](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1218/)], such as [PsExec](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/software/S0029/) [[T1021.002](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1021/002/)] and Windows Remote Management (WinRM) [[T1021.006](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1021/006/)], to execute commands on remote systems. In several instances, SafePay threat actors used `ShareFinder.ps1`—a PowerShell script function used for enumerating network file shares in Windows Active Directory environments—to identify valuable assets for exfiltration [[T1059.001](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001/), [T1059.003](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/003/)]. Additionally, SafePay threat actors used `SharpShares.exe`, a tool used to identify and enumerate network file shares.

As SafePay threat actors prepare for lateral movement, they commonly disable security software [[T1685](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1685/ "T1685")] to avoid detection. In some cases, the threat actors changed the passwords of system users, making valid logins impossible. SafePay threat actors have also been observed using software packing or virtual machine software protection to conceal their code [[T1027.002](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1027/002/ "T1027.002")].

### **Privilege Escalation**

The authoring agencies observed SafePay threat actors using a User Account Control (UAC) Bypass Privilege Escalation technique [[T1548.002](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1548/002/)] that creates an elevated process through a specified component object model, enabling the execution of malicious commands or binaries [[T1059](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1059/)]. SafePay threat actors have also been observed using token impersonation methods [[T1134.001](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1134/001/)].

### **Lateral Movement**

To pivot laterally through a network and identify files for exfiltration [[TA0010](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/tactics/TA0010/)] and encryption, SafePay threat actors use legitimate tools like PsExec, WinRM, and Remote Management and Monitoring [[T1569.002](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1569/002/)] software. They also employ Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) [[T1021.001](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1021/001/), [T1133](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1133/)] to expand their presence within the compromised network [[T1021](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1021/)]. As SafePay threat actors move laterally through a network, they look for credentials to gain admin access and will create accounts, if needed.

### **Collection, Exfiltration, and Impact**

SafePay threat actors use legitimate tools, such as FileZilla, 7-Zip, RClone, and WinRAR to compress files and collect data [[T1560.001](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1560/001/)], and WinSCP and RClone to facilitate the exfiltration of data [[T1048](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1048/)] from the compromised network to actor-controlled accounts. In some instances, SafePay threat actors use OneDrive to exfiltrate data [[T1567](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1567/)]. In other instances, the SafePay threat actors installed legitimate remote desktop tools like AnyDesk or TeamViewer on the victim’s systems. SafePay threat actors use these tools to transfer ransomware and other software onto a victim’s system. Additionally, SafePay threat actors halt the Volume Shadow Copy Service and reset Network Attached Storage containing victim backup data to initial factory settings to inhibit system recovery attempts [[T1490](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1490/)].

Following exfiltration, SafePay threat actors encrypt files [[T1486](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1486/ "T1486")] using a sophisticated hybrid encryption scheme to lock data. This involves combining a ChaCha20 stream cipher with an RSA public-key cryptosystem for a fast and secure key exchange. This multilayered approach customizes encryption methods based on file type and size and enables full or partial encryption. Data encrypted with the SafePay ransomware variant (appended with `.SafePay`) cannot be recovered without the decryption key. SafePay threat actors hold the encrypted data for ransom and typically demand a subset of company revenue in cryptocurrency in exchange for decryption software. SafePay threat actors leave a ransom note on the victim’s system, typically on the desktop or root directory, advising them that SafePay locked their system and to reach out over a chat portal at a TOR `.onion` website link with a victim ID as the login. To put pressure on the victim, a representative makes phone calls on behalf of the ransomware group. If the victim does not pay, SafePay posts the exfiltrated data to their SafePay leak website, which is linked in the ransom note. Following encryption, SafePay may delete itself from the victim’s filesystem [[T1070.004](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1070/004/ "T1070.004")].

### **Leveraged Tools**

[**Table 1**](https://www.cisa.gov/cybersecurity-advisories/all.xml#Table1)lists publicly available tools and applications used by SafePay threat actors, including legitimate tools repurposed for their operations.  
**Note:**These tools and applications should not be attributed as malicious without analytical evidence to support threat actor use and/or control.

[](https://www.cisa.gov/cybersecurity-advisories/all.xml)_**Table 1. Tools Leveraged by SafePay Threat Actors**_
| Tool Name | Description |
| --- | --- |
| 7-Zip | An open source software that enables users to compress various file formats. SafePay threat actors maliciously use 7-Zip for data packaging and movement. |
| FileZilla | An open source client that enables users to transfer files between servers. SafePay threat actors use FileZilla maliciously for data exfiltration. |
| Mimikatz | A credential dumper that enables users to view and save authentication credentials, such as Kerberos tickets [[T1550.002](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1550/002/ "T1550.002")]. SafePay threat actors use Mimikatz for password exfiltration. |
| PowerShell | A cross-platform task automation solution used for various malicious activities, including command execution. It is composed of a command-line shell, a scripting language, and a configuration management framework—which runs on Windows, Linux, and macOS. |
| PsExec | A tool designed to run programs that SafePay threat actors use to execute commands on remote systems. |
| RClone | A legitimate command-line program used to sync files with cloud storage services, such as Mega. SafePay threat actors maliciously use RClone for data packaging and movement. |
| ScreenConnect | A remote support, access, and meeting software that allows users to control devices remotely over the internet. SafePay threat actors use ScreenConnect for persistence and access. |
| ShareFinder.ps1 | A PowerShell script used to scan an Active Directory network for shared folders. SafePay threat actors use `ShareFinder.ps1` for exfiltration hunting. |
| WinRAR | Archive utility used to split compromised data into segments and to compress [[T1560.001](https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v20/techniques/T1560/001/ "T1560.001")] files into a `.RAR` format prior to exfiltration. SafePay threat actors use WinRAR for data packaging and movement. |
| WinRM | A Windows-based protocol designed to facilitate remote management and automation. SafePay threat actors use WinRM for command execution. |
| AnyDesk and TeamViewer | Legitimate remote desktop tool used for accessing and controlling computers over the internet. SafePay threat actors use AnyDesk and TeamViewer to transfer malware to the victim’s system. |
| AcronisDiskDirector | A legitimate disk management tool. |
| SharpShares.exe | A network scanning tool used for host discovery and enumeration. |
| NetExec | A legitimate network assessment tool to enumerate systems, validate credentials, and manage remote operations across private networks. |
| Backblaze | A command-line tool for file management within Backblaze B2 cloud storage. SafePay threat actors executed commands via `b2.exe` to exfiltrate a victim’s data. |

